# worktree-sweep

The counterpart to `worktree-task`. That skill creates one worktree and tears down
the one you are standing in; this one sweeps every worktree on the machine that no
longer has a reason to exist.

```bash
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py            # audit, change nothing
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --apply    # audit, then remove SWEEP-verdict worktrees
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --repo trade-agents --apply
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --json     # machine-readable
```

Removals are automatic under `--apply` — no per-worktree confirmation. That is
deliberate: every gate below must pass first, and a swept worktree is one
`git worktree add` from being recreated. Every removal is appended to
`~/.claude/worktree-sweep/sweep.log`.

The **local branch ref is kept** unless you pass `--prune-branches`. GitHub deletes
the remote branch on merge (verified: `amont` `fix/trust-per-worktree` 404s after
merge) while Forgejo keeps it (`default_delete_branch_after_merge: false`), so on
GitHub repos the local ref can be the last *named* handle on the pre-squash commits.
They stay reachable either way — in the object store and permanently as
`refs/pull/<n>/head` — but a branch ref is free and a 1 GB worktree is not.

## Content gate — SWEEP without a PR (added 2026-10-14)

The person's rule: *if the changes are on origin main, it's safe to delete.*
A worktree, detached or on a branch, earns SWEEP by content alone when:

- its HEAD is an ancestor of `origin/<base>` (after the per-repo fetch), so
  removing it loses no commit;
- the tree is clean, except for uncommitted edits to `CLAUDE.md` /
  `AGENTS.md`, which are generated guidance and are restored (`git restore`)
  just before removal, logged as `DROPPED`;
- its HEAD has not moved for 24 h (`MIN_IDLE_HOURS`, read from the
  worktree's reflog, never the index). A task that just ran `worktree add -b
  feat/x origin/main` has a HEAD on base and no edits yet; this guards it.

No forge call, no release gate: nothing in the worktree is unmerged. A
branch that fails the content gate goes on to the PR gates below; a detached
HEAD that fails it is held ("not on origin/<base>"). Before this gate, ten
such worktrees (2.9 GB) were held only for being detached.

## PR gates — for work that is not on base yet; all must pass

1. Not the primary checkout, not the current working directory, not `locked`,
   no `.no-sweep` marker file inside it.
2. On a real branch that is not the base branch (a detached HEAD is decided
   by the content gate only).
3. No modified and no untracked files, save `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md` edits.
4. Has an upstream, and zero commits ahead of it (nothing unpushed).
5. A pull request for that branch exists and is **merged**.
6. A `v*` tag contains the merge commit — *unless the repo has no `v*` tags at
   all*, in which case merge is the release (see homelab below).

## The one thing not to break

A forge that cannot be reached yields **UNKNOWN**, and UNKNOWN never removes
anything. This is not paranoia, it is the observed failure mode of both forges:

- `gh` on an API timeout prints nothing and the naive read is "no PR exists".
  Measured: `cluster-vision` reported no PR on one run and `#14 MERGED` on the
  next; `amont#232` reported an empty merge commit while sitting on `main` as
  `1288b4d`.
- Forgejo's `/pulls` endpoint returns the **repo object** — a dict, not a list —
  when given the unsupported `state=all`. Parsed loosely, that is an empty PR
  list. `pr_forgejo` therefore requires an integer `number` *and* a `head` key
  before it believes it has a PR.

For a *keep* decision a false negative is harmless; for a *delete* decision it is
data loss. Never "optimise" an UNKNOWN into a HOLD-equivalent that proceeds.

## Forge specifics

- **GitHub** (`fredericrous/*`): `gh pr list --head <branch> --state all`.
- **Forgejo** (`{{ .forge.host }}`): needs *both* the mTLS client cert and the
  `~/.netrc` token (see `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`). Use `GET /pulls/{base}/{head}` —
  one request per branch, ~14s. Do **not** page `/pulls?state=closed`; against
  this forge that times out, which is how the three "missing" homelab PRs were
  first mis-read as absent. That endpoint is keyed on the base branch, so a
  **stacked PR** (base = another feature branch, e.g. homelab#688 → #687's
  branch) 404s; on a 404 the tool consults `/pulls?state=open` (fast, cached
  once per repo) by head before believing "no PR". A PR merged into a feature
  branch is a HOLD, not a SWEEP — nothing reached `main`.
- **Refs are fetched first.** One `git fetch --prune` per repo runs before any
  ahead-count. GitHub deletes the branch on merge, so without the prune the
  stale `origin/<branch>` sits at the pre-squash commit and finished work reads
  "1 unpushed commit". A branch with no surviving remote ref is proven pushed
  either by being an ancestor of `origin/<base>` or by the merged PR's head sha
  equalling the worktree HEAD; otherwise it holds as "never pushed".
- `--lookup <repo> <branch>` prints the raw forge answer for one branch — use
  it to interrogate a verdict you don't believe.
- `default_delete_branch_after_merge` is **false** on the Forgejo repos, so a
  surviving remote branch says nothing about merge state.
- Both forges squash-merge, so `git merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD origin/main`
  is useless here — a merged branch's commits never appear on `main`. The PR API
  is the only reliable signal, and `git branch -d` will always refuse (which is why
  `--prune-branches` uses `-D`, gated on a confirmed merge).

## Release gate

Repos ship on `v*` tags, so a merged PR is not yet deployed and the tool holds
until a tag contains the merge commit. **homelab has zero `v*` tags** — it ships
via Flux on merge — so for any repo with no `v*` tags the gate is skipped and
merge counts as released. Without that carve-out homelab worktrees would never
be sweepable.

## amont push rehearsal leftovers

amont checks the pushed tip out at `$TMPDIR/amont-push-<pid>-<16 hex>`
(`worktree add --detach`) and removes it when the push ends. A registered one
is detached, so gate 2 would hold it forever; instead it is SWEEP when its pid
is dead, it is over an hour old, its tree is clean, and its HEAD is still on a
ref of the repo. A live pid means a rehearsal in flight: two seen on 2026-10-07
had vanished minutes later.

## `--repo` amends the snapshot

`--repo X` replaces only X's entries in `state.json` and keeps the previous
timestamp. It used to overwrite the whole snapshot with X alone, leaving
`--nudge` blind to every other repo.

## Where worktrees live

Primaries are found under `~/Developer`; their linked worktrees are found
**wherever they live** (`/tmp`, `$TMPDIR`, a Claude scratchpad), because
`git worktree list` knows them all. The SessionStart `--nudge` counts the same
set offline, by reading each primary's `.git/worktrees/*/gitdir` registrations.

## Claude scratch session dirs (`--scratch`)

```bash
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --scratch            # audit
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --scratch --apply    # remove SWEEP dirs
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --scratch --scratch-days 3
```

Claude Code keeps per-session temp state at `<root>/<project slug>/<session uuid>/`
(`scratchpad/`, `tasks/`). `<root>` is `$CLAUDE_CODE_TMPDIR/claude-<uid>`,
defaulting to `/tmp/claude-<uid>` — not `$TMPDIR`. `--scratch-root` overrides it.
Only UUID-named session dirs are judged: the root also holds Claude Code's own
state (`bash-edit-diff/`, `node-compile-cache/`, `bundled-skills/`), never touched.

A session dir is SWEEP only when all hold:

1. Not the running session (`$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`).
2. Idle ≥ N days (default 7), by the transcript
   `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<uuid>.jsonl` and the newest *file* inside.
   **Directory mtimes are ignored**: something prunes these dirs nightly and
   every deletion stamps the parent at 00:00, making 3-day-idle sessions look
   active today.
3. No linked worktree inside (that is the worktree sweep's job), and no repo
   inside that is the primary of a live worktree elsewhere.
4. Every repo inside that **has a remote** is clean with no commit absent from
   its remotes. A remote-less repo was born in the scratchpad — scratch by
   construction — and so is a `.git` gutted by the pruner (no `HEAD`, no `config`).

A git command that fails yields UNKNOWN, which is never removed. Removals are
logged as `SCRATCH-REMOVED` in `sweep.log`.

## Never

- Never pass `--force` to `git worktree remove`. If it refuses because of an
  untracked file, that file is the point — inspect it.
- Never sweep on the strength of a local heuristic (branch merged into main,
  remote branch deleted, commit subject matching a squash commit). Ask the forge.
